glue: a port that does not trap can still compile

sigsegv_handler() refuses to build without HAVE_SIGSEGV_SKIP_INSTRUCTION,
with an #error in the middle of the function. The intent is sound — a
port that traps has to be able to step over the faulting instruction, and
silently doing nothing would be worse — but it also stops a port that
does not trap at all.

That is Okapia's case. It routes the Macintosh's device accesses to
memory rather than letting them fault, so there is no fault to recover
from and nothing to skip. Anything that did reach this handler would be
real, and the report below the #if is exactly the right answer to it.

Two declarations move above the conditional so the fall-through has the
program counter it prints, and the #error becomes that fall-through. No
existing build changes: every host that defines the capability still
takes the same path it did.

Okapia (planification.md §19.4). Offered upstream: an #error inside a
function is hostile to a port whose answer is "this cannot happen".

---
diff --git a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/sheepshaver_glue.cpp b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/sheepshaver_glue.cpp
index 7f857920..e6ec8b56 100755
--- a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/sheepshaver_glue.cpp
+++ b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/sheepshaver_glue.cpp
@@ -775,14 +775,15 @@ sigsegv_return_t sigsegv_handler(sigsegv_info_t *sip)
 #endif
 
 	const uintptr addr = (uintptr)sigsegv_get_fault_address(sip);
-#if HAVE_SIGSEGV_SKIP_INSTRUCTION
-	// Ignore writes to ROM
-	if ((addr - (uintptr)ROMBaseHost) < ROM_SIZE)
-		return SIGSEGV_RETURN_SKIP_INSTRUCTION;
 
 	// Get program counter of target CPU
 	sheepshaver_cpu * const cpu = ppc_cpu;
 	const uint32 pc = cpu->pc();
+
+#if HAVE_SIGSEGV_SKIP_INSTRUCTION
+	// Ignore writes to ROM
+	if ((addr - (uintptr)ROMBaseHost) < ROM_SIZE)
+		return SIGSEGV_RETURN_SKIP_INSTRUCTION;
 	
 	// Fault in Mac ROM or RAM?
 	bool mac_fault = (pc >= ROMBase && pc < (ROMBase + ROM_AREA_SIZE)) || (pc >= RAMBase && pc < (RAMBase + RAMSize)) || (pc >= DR_CACHE_BASE && pc < (DR_CACHE_BASE + DR_CACHE_SIZE));
@@ -821,7 +822,10 @@ sigsegv_return_t sigsegv_handler(sigsegv_info_t *sip)
 			return SIGSEGV_RETURN_SKIP_INSTRUCTION;
 	}
 #else
-#error "FIXME: You don't have the capability to skip instruction within signal handlers"
+	// Nothing to skip with. A port that routes device accesses to memory
+	// instead of trapping on them has no faults to recover from either, so
+	// anything arriving here is real: fall through to the report below.
+	(void)addr;
 #endif
 
 	fprintf(stderr, "SIGSEGV\n");
